 "check_text": "protocol.py: 1 biology problem (1 note).\n\n1. NOTE CONTAM-WELL: A used tip carried diluent (tube A1 in slot 7) into well A1 in slot 4\nit was never meant for\n   line 50:  p1000.aspirate(250, prev_well)\n   At line 50 the tip touched the liquid in well A1 in slot 4 while it still carried diluent\n   (tube A1 in slot 7) from an earlier step. A tip that has touched one liquid leaves a\n   little of it in the next liquid it touches. The protocol never transfers that liquid into\n   this well on purpose, so it was not meant to be there. If the wells hold different\n   samples, each now holds a little of another. Liquid was later taken out of the affected\n   wells, and the unwanted material went along into 2 other wells: wells A1 and A2 in slot\n   6.\n   Affected: well A1 in slot 4.\n   Also reached 2 other wells: wells A1 and A2 in slot 6.\n   Fix: Use a fresh tip for each well at line 50. If one tip must serve many wells, dispense\n   from above the liquid (for example `well.top()`) so that the tip never touches what is\n   already in the well.\n   Source: Lab Manager: minimizing cross-contamination in automated liquid handling\n           https://www.labmanager.com/minimizing-cross-contamination-in-automated-liquid-handling-workflows-35194\n   [bd8c246f73d8]\n\nThe Opentrons simulator (8.8.2) passed this protocol with no error: it checks that the robot\ncan make the moves, not what they do to the liquids.",
